1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,490
You are seven years old.

2
00:00:01,790 --> 00:00:04,633
You're inside a chimney, in total darkness. The tunnel around you is

3
00:00:04,633 --> 00:00:07,476
about as wide as a dinner plate, and your knees are pressed

4
00:00:07,476 --> 00:00:10,319
up against your chin.

5
00:00:10,619 --> 00:00:13,319
You can't go up. You can't go down.

6
00:00:13,619 --> 00:00:16,870
And here's the twist. A law already says you shouldn't be here.

7
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:20,120
Everyone knows it. Nobody cares.

8
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:22,975
So how did a seven year old end up inside a wall?

9
00:00:22,975 --> 00:00:25,529
Let's go back to the day you were born.

10
00:00:25,829 --> 00:00:29,176
London in the eighteen sixties is the biggest city on the planet.

11
00:00:29,176 --> 00:00:32,523
Around three million people, and almost every single one of them heats

12
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:35,870
their home and cooks their food with coal.

13
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:40,846
Coal fires mean smoke. Smoke means soot. And soot builds up inside

14
00:00:40,846 --> 00:00:45,523
chimneys until it catches fire, or the whole house fills with black

15
00:00:45,523 --> 00:00:50,200
smoke. So somebody has to clean them. Hundreds of thousands of chimneys.

16
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,225
And the cheapest chimney cleaning tool in Victorian London isn't a brush.

17
00:00:54,225 --> 00:00:57,950
It's a child. Specifically, you.

18
00:00:58,250 --> 00:01:01,870
London, eighteen sixty-two. You arrive in a single rented room, shared with

19
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:05,490
your parents, four siblings,

20
00:01:05,790 --> 00:01:07,849
and a damp patch on the wall that has more personality than

21
00:01:07,849 --> 00:01:09,909
your dad.

22
00:01:10,209 --> 00:01:14,164
Fun fact. Twenty-two years before you were born, a law already said

23
00:01:14,164 --> 00:01:18,120
nobody under twenty-one is allowed to climb a chimney.

24
00:01:18,420 --> 00:01:21,530
Hold on to that. It's going to be hilarious later.

25
00:01:21,829 --> 00:01:24,704
Your family is poor. Dinner is bread. On a good day, dinner

26
00:01:24,704 --> 00:01:27,579
is also bread.

27
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:30,043
And by the time you can walk, your parents are looking at

28
00:01:30,043 --> 00:01:32,206
you the way people look at an old coat. Maybe there's some

29
00:01:32,206 --> 00:01:34,370
money in that.

30
00:01:34,670 --> 00:01:38,596
And to be fair to your parents, there aren't many options. There's

31
00:01:38,596 --> 00:01:42,523
no free school yet. No child benefits. No safety net. If the

32
00:01:42,523 --> 00:01:46,450
family can't eat, somebody small is going to work.

33
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:50,329
Then one morning, a man in a black top hat knocks on

34
00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:53,909
the door. He's a master sweep, and he likes what he sees.

35
00:01:54,209 --> 00:01:56,320
You're small. You're skinny.

36
00:01:56,620 --> 00:01:58,700
You look like you'd fit in a drainpipe. In this job, that's

37
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:00,780
a compliment.

38
00:02:01,079 --> 00:02:04,284
Kids like you were often orphans, or workhouse boys, or simply handed

39
00:02:04,284 --> 00:02:07,490
over by parents who needed the money.

40
00:02:07,790 --> 00:02:12,599
Some climbing boys started as young as four. Most were somewhere between

41
00:02:12,599 --> 00:02:17,409
seven and ten. Old enough to follow orders. Small enough to fit.

42
00:02:17,710 --> 00:02:22,579
The paperwork calls it an apprenticeship. Your mum calls it an opportunity.

43
00:02:22,879 --> 00:02:25,869
You call it, why is that man holding my arm.

44
00:02:26,169 --> 00:02:29,289
And that's it. No goodbye party. No backpack. You walk down the

45
00:02:29,289 --> 00:02:32,409
street behind a stranger, and your childhood stays behind in that room

46
00:02:32,409 --> 00:02:35,530
with the damp patch.

47
00:02:35,830 --> 00:02:38,466
Your new home is the master's cellar. Your bed is a pile

48
00:02:38,466 --> 00:02:41,103
of soot bags. A mattress made of the exact stuff you're paid

49
00:02:41,103 --> 00:02:43,739
to remove.

50
00:02:44,039 --> 00:02:47,189
The workday starts early. Sometimes your master drags you out of bed

51
00:02:47,189 --> 00:02:50,339
at three in the morning, because rich people want clean chimneys before

52
00:02:50,339 --> 00:02:53,489
they light the morning fires.

53
00:02:53,789 --> 00:02:57,949
Then comes training. There's no school. No safety talk.

54
00:02:58,250 --> 00:03:00,409
Here's the chimney. Up you go.

55
00:03:00,710 --> 00:03:05,039
Fun fact about rich people's chimneys. The fancier the house, the more

56
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:09,370
complicated the flues. They bend. They twist. They split into different rooms.

57
00:03:09,370 --> 00:03:13,699
Rich people liked architecture. You will learn to hate it.

58
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:17,539
Inside, the flue can narrow to about nine inches by nine inches.

59
00:03:17,539 --> 00:03:21,079
Smaller than this video on a laptop screen. No ladder. No rope.

60
00:03:21,079 --> 00:03:24,619
Just you.

61
00:03:24,919 --> 00:03:28,224
You push up with your knees and elbows. Not a style choice.

62
00:03:28,224 --> 00:03:31,530
There's simply no room to do anything else.

63
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:33,745
By the end of week one, your knees and elbows are scraped

64
00:03:33,745 --> 00:03:35,659
raw.

65
00:03:35,960 --> 00:03:38,389
By the end of week two, they're still raw. They just stop

66
00:03:38,389 --> 00:03:40,819
surprising you.

67
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:44,756
Between jobs, climbing boys walked the streets shouting out for work. Sweep!

68
00:03:44,756 --> 00:03:48,393
Sweep! Basically a walking advert, at six in the morning, for a

69
00:03:48,393 --> 00:03:52,030
service where the product is you.

70
00:03:52,330 --> 00:03:55,629
And if you think your knees will eventually toughen up? They do.

71
00:03:55,629 --> 00:03:58,930
Eventually. After weeks of scraping. Some masters had their own methods to

72
00:03:58,930 --> 00:04:02,230
speed that up. You do not want to know them. And honestly,

73
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:05,530
neither does YouTube.

74
00:04:05,830 --> 00:04:08,805
Days turn into a rhythm. Wake up in the dark. Climb in

75
00:04:08,805 --> 00:04:11,780
the dark. Breathe soot in the dark.

76
00:04:12,080 --> 00:04:15,225
But sometimes, at the very top, you poke your head out of

77
00:04:15,225 --> 00:04:18,370
a chimney pot and see the whole of London at sunrise.

78
00:04:18,670 --> 00:04:23,529
And honestly? It's beautiful. It's the only window seat you'll ever get.

79
00:04:23,829 --> 00:04:25,679
Then you climb back down, and the master points at the next

80
00:04:25,679 --> 00:04:27,529
one.

81
00:04:27,829 --> 00:04:31,659
Washing? Not really. Climbing boys were rarely washed. The soot settles into

82
00:04:31,659 --> 00:04:35,490
every fold of your skin, and it stays there.

83
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:37,995
Remember that. It comes back at the end, and it does not

84
00:04:37,995 --> 00:04:40,199
come back nicely.

85
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:43,816
Meanwhile, your master gets paid for every chimney. You get food, a

86
00:04:43,816 --> 00:04:47,133
roof made of soot bags, and occasionally a coin if he's in

87
00:04:47,133 --> 00:04:50,449
a good mood. He's never in a good mood.

88
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:53,240
Now here's the part that will make you angry.

89
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:58,350
A mechanical brush, a long jointed pole with a brush on the

90
00:04:58,350 --> 00:05:03,159
end, had existed since around eighteen-oh-three. Before your grandparents were even married.

91
00:05:03,459 --> 00:05:05,659
So why are you still climbing?

92
00:05:05,959 --> 00:05:09,870
Because plenty of sweeps and their customers simply preferred boys. Boys were

93
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:13,779
cheap, and boys didn't complain to anyone important.

94
00:05:14,079 --> 00:05:17,659
You are, technically, a more expensive option than a stick. You're also

95
00:05:17,659 --> 00:05:21,240
a more convenient one. Which is worse.

96
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:25,066
Imagine being a rich lady who looks at a brush that works

97
00:05:25,066 --> 00:05:28,593
perfectly, and then at a starving seven year old, and thinks, yes,

98
00:05:28,593 --> 00:05:32,120
the child please. That's the energy of the eighteen hundreds.

99
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:36,955
In eighteen sixty-four, Parliament passes another Chimney Sweepers Act. Fines and everything.

100
00:05:36,955 --> 00:05:41,490
Climbing boys are now extra illegal.

101
00:05:41,790 --> 00:05:43,409
And everyone ignores it.

102
00:05:43,709 --> 00:05:47,384
Around this time, a writer called Charles Kingsley publishes a children's book

103
00:05:47,384 --> 00:05:51,059
called The Water-Babies. Its hero is a climbing boy named Tom. Readers

104
00:05:51,059 --> 00:05:54,734
across Britain are horrified. People who never once looked inside a chimney

105
00:05:54,734 --> 00:05:58,409
suddenly care.

106
00:05:58,709 --> 00:06:02,263
And still, a book can change minds. It can't open locked doors.

107
00:06:02,263 --> 00:06:05,816
Masters keep sending boys up. Customers keep paying. The law becomes more

108
00:06:05,816 --> 00:06:09,370
of a suggestion.

109
00:06:09,670 --> 00:06:13,685
You're nine. A man in Parliament has decided you shouldn't be here.

110
00:06:13,685 --> 00:06:17,699
Your master has decided Parliament can't see inside a chimney.

111
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,620
Your master is right.

112
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:22,653
Which brings us back to where we started. A flue that bends.

113
00:06:22,653 --> 00:06:25,386
Knees jammed against your chin. You can't go up, and you can't

114
00:06:25,386 --> 00:06:28,120
go down.

115
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:32,810
This happened to climbing boys again and again. When it ended badly,

116
00:06:32,810 --> 00:06:37,199
the coroner often wrote, accidental death. Nobody went to jail.

117
00:06:37,500 --> 00:06:40,579
Today, you're lucky. Your master breaks a few bricks out of the

118
00:06:40,579 --> 00:06:43,659
wall and pulls you out by the ankles.

119
00:06:43,959 --> 00:06:46,745
He's furious. Not because you almost died. Because now he has to

120
00:06:46,745 --> 00:06:49,529
pay for the wall.

121
00:06:49,829 --> 00:06:53,009
And here's the dark part. For you, this was a lucky day.

122
00:06:53,009 --> 00:06:56,189
Many climbing boys stuck in a chimney never got a hammer and

123
00:06:56,189 --> 00:06:59,370
a pair of hands. They got the coroner's book.

124
00:06:59,670 --> 00:07:02,685
February, eighteen seventy-five. You're thirteen now, and getting too big for the

125
00:07:02,685 --> 00:07:05,699
flues.

126
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,819
Then the newspapers start talking about a boy called George Brewster.

127
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:13,212
George was a climbing boy too. On the eleventh of February, he

128
00:07:13,212 --> 00:07:16,305
was sent up a chimney at the asylum in Fulbourn, near Cambridge.

129
00:07:16,305 --> 00:07:19,397
He got stuck. By the time they pulled him out, it was

130
00:07:19,397 --> 00:07:22,490
too late.

131
00:07:22,790 --> 00:07:25,370
His master was charged with manslaughter.

132
00:07:25,670 --> 00:07:30,529
Lord Shaftesbury reads about George, and pushes one more law through Parliament.

133
00:07:30,829 --> 00:07:33,639
This time, sweeps need a licence from the police. And the police

134
00:07:33,639 --> 00:07:36,449
actually have to enforce it.

135
00:07:36,750 --> 00:07:40,079
By September eighteen seventy-five, the climbing boy era in Britain is basically

136
00:07:40,079 --> 00:07:43,410
over. George is often remembered as the last climbing boy to die

137
00:07:43,410 --> 00:07:46,740
in England.

138
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:49,600
You survived it. You're one of the lucky ones. Which is a

139
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:52,159
really low bar.

140
00:07:52,459 --> 00:07:56,053
And honestly, for a while, life gets better. You're bigger, you're free

141
00:07:56,053 --> 00:07:59,646
of the flues, and you find work above ground. You can see

142
00:07:59,646 --> 00:08:03,240
the sky every day. You forgot how much you missed it.

143
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:05,830
Years later, you notice a small wart. In a place you'd rather

144
00:08:05,830 --> 00:08:08,120
not point at.

145
00:08:08,420 --> 00:08:11,875
Sweeps had a name for it. The soot wart. In seventeen seventy-five,

146
00:08:11,875 --> 00:08:15,330
almost a hundred years before your first climb, a surgeon called Percivall

147
00:08:15,330 --> 00:08:18,785
Pott worked out it was cancer, caused by soot sitting in the

148
00:08:18,785 --> 00:08:22,240
folds of a sweep's skin.

149
00:08:22,540 --> 00:08:25,990
It's known as the first cancer ever linked to someone's job.

150
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:30,579
Everybody knew. And the soot bags got used as beds anyway.

151
00:08:30,879 --> 00:08:33,723
So in the end, your career didn't just steal your childhood. It

152
00:08:33,723 --> 00:08:36,566
sent you a bill, decades later, for every night you slept on

153
00:08:36,566 --> 00:08:39,410
those bags.

154
00:08:39,710 --> 00:08:42,776
So that's your life. Born into a room with a damp patch.

155
00:08:42,776 --> 00:08:45,843
Sold to a man in a top hat. Sent up chimneys that

156
00:08:45,843 --> 00:08:48,910
a law had already banned.

157
00:08:49,210 --> 00:08:50,935
And the whole time, a brush on a stick could have done

158
00:08:50,935 --> 00:08:52,660
your job.

159
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,626
Thanks for living it with us. Next time, you're born as a

160
00:08:55,626 --> 00:08:58,293
medieval executioner. Everyone needs you. Nobody will sit next to you. Hit

161
00:08:58,293 --> 00:09:00,960
subscribe, and pick your next past life.
